  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 249 AM CST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nFog continues to expand across parts of northwestern and central \nNorth Dakota, though the densest fog remains fairly patchy for \nnow. Therefore, the ongoing Dense Fog Advisory looks good at \nthis time. Also issued an SPS along peripheral counties, as well\nas western McLean County, until 5 am CST. Will re-evaluate \nthen. Other than in the northwest, fog seems relatively \ntransitory, so will see how long it lasts for at any particular \nlocation. Conditions should improve by mid to late morning, \nthough redevelopment is possible again tonight in western North \nDakota. While southeasterly surface flow is expected, these \nwinds, along with near-surface winds, might be just a tad too \nstrong for widespread dense fog development and could instead \nfavor low-level stratus with limited visibility reductions.\n\nOther than fog development, quiet weather remains in the\nforecast through this weekend and into early next week. For\ntoday, a ridge over the eastern Rockies/High Plains will\ncontinue to amplify resulting in northwesterly flow aloft\nfollowed by northerly flow across the state Thursday and \nFriday. This will result in moderately sharp temperature \ngradients over the next few days with northern and eastern North\nDakota being on the cool side of the ridge today, and all but \nwestern North Dakota being on the cooler side Thursday and \nFriday. Generally speaking, areas on the coolest side will see \nhighs in the mid 30s with those on the warmest side reaching the\nlow to mid 50s. A gradual warming trend is then on tap this \nweekend into early next week for locations that were on the \ncooler side of the ridge. By Tuesday, the NBM currently brings \nthe entire state into the 50s.\n\nThe one other thing worth noting at this time is winds. Surface\nhigh pressure over central Canada will gradually slide down \ntowards western Ontario and into Minnesota by the end of the \nworkweek. As this occurs, low pressure will also develop along\nthe lee of the Northern Rockies. The tightening pressure \ngradient from these two pressure centers will likely result in \nbreezy to windy conditions in western North Dakota on Friday, \nand then across most of the state on Saturday with some stronger\ngusts possible in western North Dakota.",
